INT. CASSETTE ARCHIVE - BASEMENT OFFICE - NIGHT

A low room of grey metal shelves holding thousands of labeled tapes. A bare bulb swings slightly, though no wind reaches down here. Dust and old magnetic oxide. A reel-to-reel and a cassette deck sit side by side on a cluttered desk beneath a corkboard of photographs strung together with red thread.

EVANGELINE ROTHCHILD-ADU, 50s, weary detective in a green cardigan, sits at the desk. Her hair is pinned with a pencil. She holds a microphone close to her lips, as though it were a confidant.

She presses record.

EVANGELINE
Case file nine-one-four. The Hargreave matter. Evening, third of November.

She lets go of the button. Picks up a photograph. Puts it down. Presses again.

EVANGELINE (CONT'D)
They told me the library caught fire by accident. I have read eleven reports that say so. Not one of them mentions the smell of kerosene, which three witnesses described independently.

She taps a thread on the corkboard.

EVANGELINE (CONT'D)
The librarian, Mr. Quillfeather, vanished that night. His wife says he never returned. His employer says he resigned a week earlier. Both cannot be true.

She rewinds the cassette. Plays it back.

EVANGELINE (V.O.)
(recorded, thin)
...three witnesses described independently...

She stops it. Writes on a pad.

EVANGELINE
I found his key in the ash. A small brass key. It opens a locker in the basement of this very building. I've checked every locker. There isn't one.

She leans back. Rubs her eyes.

EVANGELINE (CONT'D)
Unless the basement I've been searching is the wrong basement.

From somewhere in the stacks, a tape clicks and begins to turn.

She goes still. Slowly lowers the microphone.

A voice fills the room from a distant shelf, on tape, calm and a little amused. It is her own.

EVANGELINE (V.O.)
(recorded, distant)
If you're hearing this, Evangeline, you've finally found the right basement.

She stands. The chair rolls back into a shelf, scattering tapes.

EVANGELINE
That's my voice.

EVANGELINE (V.O.)
(recorded)
Third shelf on your left. Behind the tapes labeled Nineteen Seventy-Two.

Evangeline crosses. Her heels click. She pulls out box after box until a small brass-hinged door appears in the wall. She takes the key from her cardigan pocket. Her hand shakes. She turns it.

The door opens. Inside, nothing but a single reel marked: PLAY LAST.
